{"step_index":101,"source":"SYSTEM","type":"SYSTEM_MESSAGE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-15T02:37:09Z","content":"The following is a <SYSTEM_MESSAGE> not actually sent by the user. It is provided by the system as important information to pay attention to.\n\n<SYSTEM_MESSAGE>\n[Message] timestamp=2026-11-15T02:37:05Z sender=649ed4c8-ed29-4715-a774-295f1681ef85 priority=MESSAGE_PRIORITY_HIGH content=COMPLETED\n**File path**: `rolapp/index.html` (in the repository root).\n\n**Button markup (≈10 lines surrounding)**:\n```html\n<!-- NavBar component (rendered inside <script type=\"text/babel\">) -->\n<nav className=\"card flex\" style={{marginBottom:\"1rem\"}}>\n  <button className=\"nav-link\" style={{marginRight:\"0.5rem\"}}\n          onClick={() => setView(\"list\")}>Personajes</button>\n  <button className=\"nav-link\" style={{marginRight:\"0.5rem\"}}\n          onClick={() => setView(\"maps\")}>Mapas</button>\n  <!-- The button of interest -->\n  <button className=\"nav-link\" onClick={() => setView(\"mapedit\")}>Crear mapa</button>\n</nav>\n```\n*The HTML file contains a single‑page React app; the button is created inside the `NavBar` component written in JSX.\n\n**`setView` definition (React state setter)**:\n```js\nfunction App(){\n  const [characters,setCharacters]=React.useState(loadChars());\n  const [maps,setMaps]=React.useState(loadMaps());\n  // <-- the view state controls which component is rendered\n  const [view,setView]=React.useState(\"list\");\n  // ...rest of the component\n}\n```\n`setView` is **not a global function**; it is the state‑updater returned by `React.useState`. It only works inside the React component tree.\n\n**Why the button produces no visible result**\n1. **Incorrect `onclick` target** – the original requirement mentions `<button class='nav-link' onclick='setView('map_edit')'>`. In the current code the button uses the React `onClick` prop (`onClick={() => setView(\"mapedit\")}`) and the view name is `\"mapedit\"`, not `\"map_edit\"`. If the HTML were rendered with a plain `onclick=\"setView('map_edit')\"`, the browser would look for a **global** `setView` function, which does not exist, so the click does nothing.\n2. **Scope mismatch** – even if a global `setView` existed, the React app expects the view identifiers `\"list\"`, `\"maps\"`, and `\"mapedit\"`. Supplying `\"map_edit\"` would not match any branch of the conditional rendering (`view===\"mapedit\"`), leaving the UI unchanged.\n3. **Result** – because the button’s click handler cannot find a matching function or view name, the UI stays on the current screen, giving the impression that nothing happened.\n\n**Fix suggestions**\n- Change the button to use the same view identifier as the app (`setView('mapedit')`).\n- If you need a plain HTML `onclick`, expose a wrapper `window.setView = (v)=>{ /* call React setter via a ref or custom event */ }`.\n\nThis extracts the requested file path, code snippets, and the explanation of the non‑functional button.\n</SYSTEM_MESSAGE>"}
